A||||7||johnt671||Steering Damper Removal  Any Problems||||5||
Z||000000||johnt671||11-16-2001||01:51 PM||||Saw in a couple of threads that removing the steering damper will lighten the steering up.  Any problems in doing this to a street driven car?  84 SE.||||reg||5
Z||000001||watts||11-16-2001||01:59 PM||||Depends on if you consider lighter steering, and better response a problem or not.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Coming out of a tight turn is great now!  Let go of the wheel and 'snap' you're going straight!<P>  Just remember never to drive with your thumbs or whatever inside the rim of the wheel.  Hard bumps that tried to 'flip' the wheel around before will now do it and attempt to remove thumbs (same rule applies when you're 4*4'ing [not in your fiero!]).<P>Only thing I could say that could be considered a problem would be that on the highway I find the front end almost too responsive now (tends to be a bit 'darty').  I also now can actually tell that my inner rack bushings are worn (it was being masked before).  Some say that your tires will wear faster - I can't see that, except that maybe you'll tend to drive more agressively and cause more wear.  I know I do!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||roadwarrior||11-16-2001||01:59 PM||||if you ask me it's not a good idea because you will get alot more bump steer, possibly enough to break your thumbs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||11-16-2001||03:47 PM||||Break your thumbs?  I drove my 84 without a steering damper from 1985 until I sold it this year.  It's been on paved and unpaved roads.  I've never even come close to having the wheel jerked out of my hands, even the time I managed to hit a curb rather hard!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnt671||11-16-2001||03:54 PM||||I may try it.  I have a piece of shrapnel in my back that really bothers me as I get older and it hurts some to turn the wheels especially when turning the wheel while stopped.  This time of year and me no longer get along.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ToddAsh||11-16-2001||06:37 PM|||| It makes all the difference.Liked my Fiero before,love it now.NO BAD SIDE .Raced a lot of years,can't see a downside.Used to wear out my arms on a long country drive.Not anymore.Do it. Cheap power steering.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||11-16-2001||07:24 PM||||When I removed the steering damper from my '85 SE, transient steering became lighter, but steering kickback increased, particularly while braking on rough pavement.  Hold on tight!<P>If your car has 14" Hi-Tech alloy wheels, you can replace the front wheels with nearly-identical Grand Am wheels.  They are the same style, diameter, and width, but their offset is different, so tires sit about 1/2" inboard, compared to Fiero wheels.  This will further lighten steering and reduce kickback.  I did this on my car, and it feels great!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnt671||11-16-2001||08:48 PM||||Sounds like a good thing to try. Think I'll do it this weekend.  Supose to be in the 60's both days. Thanks for the info.||||reg||1
